\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{A worked return to play}

\p{The route now has a concrete replay to carry its abstractions back to a
three-layer puzzle. Work in the fixed-frame HTM convention of the [complete
cube account](frap-0016): a face quarter-turn, its inverse, or a half-turn
costs one move, and words are read from left to right. Start at the solved
state and apply the declared scramble}
##{w=R\ U\ R'\ U'\ F\ R\ U\ R'\ U'\ F'.}
\p{The inverse word is}
##{w^{-1}=F\ U\ R\ U'\ R'\ F'\ U\ R\ U'\ R'.}
\p{A sticker-level replay of the six fixed-frame face maps sends the solved
state to the recorded scramble state and then returns to solved. The check
represents each of the 54 pairs in [how the cube models connect](frap-001A)
as a slot, applies the six quarter-turn permutations and their inverses, and
enumerates the projected corner states breadth first through depth two. The
token-by-token states and hashes are preserved in the [replay
receipt](https://github.com/utensil/forest/blob/main/trees/frap-001B-replay.json).
The complete cube account supplies the move convention; the replay, hashes and
262-state count are the deterministic calculation reported here. The second
replay is therefore a certificate for the move convention and the inverse,
not merely an animation of a familiar sequence.}

\subtree{
  \title{What the small search certifies}

  \p{Project the scrambled state to its corner positions and orientations.
  Exhaustive projected search finds 262 distinct projected states at depths
  zero, one, and two, while the target is absent; a projected distance of
  three is therefore a lower bound for this state. The declared word has ten
  HTM moves, so the same replay gives the bounded report}
  ##{3\le d_{\rm HTM}(w,\mathrm{id})\le 10.}
  \p{The lower bound is admissible because projection forgets edge data and
  every full move projects to one projected move. The upper bound is a valid
  word because the inverse replay reaches the target. Neither bound claims
  that ten is shortest; a full optimality claim would need exhaustive search
  through depths four through nine or a matching certificate.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{How this returns to the human method}

  \p{A player can recognize the displayed ten-move word as a repeated
  trigger pattern, but recognition is not a replacement for the state
  record. The [complete layer-first method](frap-0017) solves an arbitrary
  legal state by preserving completed layers and applying checked setup,
  trigger, and inverse-setup words; the [computer account](frap-0018) gives
  the corresponding upper-bound word and projected lower bound. This worked
  scramble shows how the two accounts meet: name the frame, replay the exact
  tokens, check the target, then state separately what has and has not been
  proved.}

\p{The example is deliberately bounded. It demonstrates a complete
scramble-and-replay loop and a concrete comparison of certificates; it does
not claim to be a full optimal solve, a universal human algorithm, or a
replacement for the all-state completeness argument.}
}
